---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import db from '../../data/power_stations.json';
import { fullName, metaFromParts, pickTitle, kgToLbs, GUIDE_URLS } from '../../lib/utils.js';
import { HEATER_LOADS, hoursOnLoad, formatSpan } from '../../lib/guides.js';

const stations = [...db.power_stations].sort((a, b) => a.capacity_wh - b.capacity_wh);
const count = stations.length;

// Space heaters require continuous rated AC wattage to deliver full heat;
// boost/voltage-drop modes starve the heating element and cut heat output.
const rows = stations.map((s) => ({
  s,
  low: hoursOnLoad(s, 750, 0.85, { useContinuousOnly: true }),
  high: hoursOnLoad(s, 1500, 0.85, { useContinuousOnly: true }),
  boostOnlyHigh: s.ac_output_w < 1500 && (s.boost_mode_w ?? 0) >= 1500,
}));

const canRunHigh = rows.filter((r) => r.high !== null);
const twoHourHigh = rows.filter((r) => r.high !== null && r.high >= 2);
const twoHourLow = rows.filter((r) => r.low !== null && r.low >= 2);

const title = pickTitle([
  `Power Station for a Space Heater: Honest Runtimes (${count} Models)`,
  'Can a Power Station Run a Space Heater? Runtimes',
]);
const description = metaFromParts([
  'A 1,500W space heater empties a 1,024Wh power station in 34 minutes.',
  `Exact 750W & 1,500W runtimes for all ${count} models, plus the boost-mode trap.`,
]);

const schema = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@graph': [
    {
      '@type': 'Article',
      headline: 'Can a power station run a space heater?',
      description,
      url: new URL(Astro.url.pathname, Astro.site).href,
      author: { '@type': 'Organization', name: 'WattLibrary' },
      publisher: { '@type': 'Organization', name: 'WattLibrary' },
      datePublished: '2026-11-26',
      dateModified: db.updated,
    },
    {
      '@type': 'BreadcrumbList',
      itemListElement: [
        { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 1, name: 'Guides', item: new URL('/guides/', Astro.site).href },
        { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 2, name: 'Power station for a space heater' },
      ],
    },
  ],
};

const faq = [
  {
    q: 'How long will a 1,000Wh power station run a 1,500W space heater?',
    a: 'About 34 minutes on High (1,500W), or about 1.1 hours on Low (750W), assuming 85% inverter efficiency. Electric resistance heating turns battery watt-hours into heat at brutal speed — there is no inverter trick around physics.',
  },
  {
    q: 'My 800W power station has 1,600W "Boost / Power Lifting" — can it run a 1,500W space heater?',
    a: 'Technically it will turn on without tripping, but it will NOT deliver 1,500W of heat. Boost modes (EcoFlow X-Boost, Bluetti Power Lifting) work by intentionally dropping AC voltage so the resistive element draws no more than the inverter\'s continuous rating (~800W). You get lukewarm low-setting heat, not full 1,500W heat.',
  },
  {
    q: 'What is a better way to stay warm on a portable power station?',
    a: 'Heat the person, not the room. An electric blanket or heated mattress pad draws 50–100W — running 8–16 hours on the same 1,024Wh battery that a space heater empties in 34 minutes. For vans or cabins, a 12V diesel heater draws only ~15–40W of electricity while burning diesel for heat.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Which portable power stations can run a space heater overnight?',
    a: 'Even on Low (750W), 8 continuous hours requires ~7,050Wh of battery capacity after inverter losses — more than any single portable unit without expansion batteries. Only expandable 3–4kWh systems (like the EcoFlow DELTA Pro 3 or Anker SOLIX F3800 Plus with extra packs) can sustain overnight electric resistance heat.',
  },
];
---
<Base {title} {description} {schema} ogType="article">
  <nav class="crumbs"><a href="/guides/">Guides</a> › Power station for a space heater</nav>
  <h1>Can a power station run a space heater?</h1>
  <p class="lead">
    Yes — briefly. Most 1,800W+ stations have no trouble powering a 1,500W space heater, which leads buyers to assume it can
    heat a room through a winter outage. The watt-hour arithmetic tells a much harsher story across all {count} models.
  </p>

  <div class="kpis">
    <div class="kpi"><b>750 W</b> typical Low setting</div>
    <div class="kpi"><b>1,500 W</b> typical High setting</div>
    <div class="kpi"><b>~34 min</b> 1,024Wh pack on High</div>
    <div class="kpi"><b>{twoHourHigh.length} of {count}</b> run High for 2+ hours</div>
  </div>

  <h2>The math no marketing page prints</h2>
  <p>
    A standard US plug-in space heater draws 1,500W continuously on High. Through an 85%-efficient inverter, a 1,024Wh power
    station delivers about 870Wh of usable AC energy — which a 1,500W heater burns through in <strong>34 minutes</strong>.
    Double the battery to a 2,048Wh unit and you still get just <strong>1.1 hours</strong> on High (or 2.3 hours on Low).
  </p>
  <div class="note">
    <b>The boost-mode trap:</b> Features like X-Boost and Power Lifting allow a 600–1,000W station to power a 1,500W heater
    without tripping overload protection — by dropping output voltage so the heater only draws the station's continuous wattage.
    That means a 1,000W station with 2,000W boost delivers ~1,000W of heat, not 1,500W. The table below tests <strong>continuous rated AC output</strong>.
  </div>

  <h2>Space heater runtimes across all {count} models</h2>
  <p class="muted">Continuous AC rating required for full-voltage heating. Rounded down at 85% inverter efficiency.</p>

  <div class="tbl tbl-sticky"><div class="tbl-scroll"><table>
    <thead>
      <tr>
        <th>Model</th>
        <th>Capacity</th>
        <th>Continuous AC</th>
        <th>Weight</th>
        {HEATER_LOADS.map((l) => <th>{l.label}</th>)}
      </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      {rows.map((r) => (
        <tr>
          <td><a href={`/power-stations/${r.s.slug}/`}>{fullName(r.s)}</a></td>
          <td>{r.s.capacity_wh.toLocaleString()} Wh</td>
          <td>{r.s.ac_output_w.toLocaleString()} W</td>
          <td>{kgToLbs(r.s.weight_kg)} lbs</td>
          <td>{r.low !== null ? formatSpan(r.low) : <span class="muted">Exceeds {r.s.ac_output_w}W</span>}</td>
          <td>
            {r.high !== null
              ? formatSpan(r.high)
              : r.boostOnlyHigh
                ? <span class="badge badge-warn">Reduced heat (boost)</span>
                : <span class="muted">Exceeds {r.s.ac_output_w}W</span>}
          </td>
        </tr>
      ))}
    </tbody>
  </table></div></div>

  <h2>What actually works for outage warmth</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Electric blanket (60–100W):</strong> Runs 8–14 hours on a standard 1,024Wh station — roughly <strong>15× longer</strong> than a space heater.</li>
    <li><strong>Gas furnace blower fan (400–600W):</strong> If your home has natural gas heat and a transfer switch, running the furnace blower on a 2–4kWh station heats the entire house for a fraction of the watt-hours of an electric space heater.</li>
    <li><strong>Short bursts on Low (750W):</strong> {twoHourLow.length} models here deliver 2+ hours on Low — useful for taking the chill off a small bathroom or tent for 20 minutes at a time, not overnight room heating.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>FAQ</h2>
  {faq.map((f) => (
    <details class="faq">
      <summary>{f.q}</summary>
      <div>{f.a}</div>
    </details>
  ))}

  <p style="margin-top:1.4rem;">
    More guides: <a href={GUIDE_URLS.fridge}>Refrigerator</a> · <a href={GUIDE_URLS.cpap}>CPAP</a> · <a href={GUIDE_URLS.solar}>Solar MPPT</a> · <a href="/calculator/">Runtime Calculator →</a>
  </p>

  <div class="disclosure">
    Estimates based on resistive loads at 750W and 1,500W with 85% inverter efficiency. Station specifications verified ({db.updated}) from manufacturer documentation — see <a href="/about/">methodology</a>.
  </div>
</Base>
